    <main class="main">
      <div class="services">
        <div class="service-item">
          <section class="section padding-2 flex vertical align-center">
            <div class="container">
              <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                <div class="_5-col">
                  <div class="relative">
                    <h2 class="h3 padding-top padding-2">Strategy</h2>
                    <div class="c_service-category-bg"><img src="/images/Vector-3.svg" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="service-content">
                <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                  <div class="_5-col">
                    <p class="rise-section-tagline">Be distinct</p>
                    <p>Brand, positioning, audience, choosing platforms, relationship with brands.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="_8-col">
                    <div class="rise-tips">
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">1. Know what you want to be known for</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Choose three subjects to return to over the next year, ideally where your clinical work, research and interest overlap, or decide you&#x27;re building a relatable persona instead of a niche.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>List the three subjects (or the persona) you&#x27;d want to be known for. Look at others you respect to see how they are doing it.</li>
                              <li>Write down one sentence on what you want from your online presence: speaking invitations, industry work, visibility or connection.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">2. Know who you are talking to</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Have a real person in your head as you post, not a follower count. A few hundred engaged cardiologists who actually listen is worth more than fifty thousand strangers scrolling past – you’re building trust with people who know your work, not chasing algorithms in the ‘creator economy’, it’s a different, better game.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Picture one real person you want to find value in your posts.</li>
                              <li>Ignore follower count for six months and track engagement rate instead, it’s a better indicator you’ve reached the right room.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">3. Tidy up your online presence</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Search your own name the way a congress programme committee would. An out-of-date bio, an old photo, a headline that&#x27;s only a job title: none of these take long to fix.</p>
                            <blockquote>82% of physicians name professional credentials as the deciding factor in trusting a healthcare voice online. <a href="https://www.sermo.com/blog/insights/mastering-the-influencer-game-in-healthcare-marketing/">[source]</a>
                            </blockquote>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Search your own name today, in an incognito browser window.</li>
                              <li>Swap in a photo taken within the last year.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">4. Choose your platform on purpose</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>LinkedIn is where peers and industry partners actually are, and where visibility turns into invitations, for most cardiologists it&#x27;s the platform that matters. CardioTwitter (X) still has real value for real-time case discussion and peer debate, that&#x27;s a different job to LinkedIn&#x27;s, not a competing one. Instagram and YouTube reach patients and reward better-made video; TikTok has huge reach but almost none of your professional audience. </p>
                            <blockquote>LinkedIn carries 78% of all B2B influencer campaign distribution, well ahead of X at 42% and YouTube at 38%. <a href="https://socioapt.com/blog/influencer-marketing-growth-statistics/">[source]</a>
                            </blockquote>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Pick one platform to commit to properly. This may well be LinkedIn.</li>
                              <li>Only add a second platform once you&#x27;re consistent on the first.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="height-2"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="divider absolute top-0"></div>
          </section>
        </div>
        <div class="service-item">
          <section class="section padding-2 flex vertical align-center">
            <div class="container">
              <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                <div class="_5-col">
                  <div class="relative">
                    <h2 class="h3 padding-top padding-2">Creative</h2>
                    <div class="c_service-category-bg"><img src="/images/Vector-3.svg" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="service-content">
                <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                  <div class="_5-col">
                    <p class="rise-section-tagline">Never run out of things to say</p>
                    <p>Ideation, sourcing ideas, content strategy.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="_8-col">
                    <div class="rise-tips">
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">5. Know where to find your next idea </h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>The questions you&#x27;re asked repeatedly are proof a topic matters. So is anything that makes you react - a new paper, someone else&#x27;s post, even a bad take you disagree with. When nothing&#x27;s landing, fall back on a shape: myth vs. fact, or the thing you got wrong early on.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Write down the last three questions you answered this week and turn one into a post.</li>
                              <li>Pick one fallback format now, before you need it.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">6. Maximise your content opportunities</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>A single talk, paper or panel can become several pieces: a video, a written post, a couple of graphics. Look at what already exists before creating from scratch.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>After every talk, panel or paper, list five formats it could become.</li>
                              <li>Keep a running “content bank” of raw material to draw on.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">7. Use YouTube and TikTok as listening tools</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Typing a phrase into either platform&#x27;s search shows how a topic is already being discussed, even if you never post there yourself.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Search your specialty on both platforms, with no plan to post.</li>
                              <li>Note the questions and phrasing people use, often different from clinical language.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">8. Don&#x27;t chase trends for their own sake</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Jumping on a trending meme, topic or sound is usually a distraction from content that actually matters, not a substitute for having something worth saying.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Check a trend fits your message before using it, skip it if not.</li>
                              <li>Spend that time instead on one useful post.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">9. Start with the hook</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Start where it gets interesting (when something went wrong, the assumption proved false, etc), not at the beginning, and fill in background only as far as the story needs.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Ask: what&#x27;s the most surprising line in this story? Start there and write that line first.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="height-2"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="divider absolute top-0"></div>
          </section>
        </div>
        <div class="service-item">
          <section class="section padding-2 flex vertical align-center">
            <div class="container">
              <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                <div class="_5-col">
                  <div class="relative">
                    <h2 class="h3 padding-top padding-2">Production</h2>
                    <div class="c_service-category-bg"><img src="/images/Vector-3.svg" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="service-content">
                <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                  <div class="_5-col">
                    <p class="rise-section-tagline">Look and sound the part</p>
                    <p>Getting the basics right, process, technical execution.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="_8-col">
                    <div class="rise-tips">
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">10. Be strict about the basics</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Shoot vertical by default, add captions since most people watch on mute, and clean the lens before filming. Proofread auto-captions too - medical terms are frequently mis-transcribed.</p>
                            <blockquote>Most social video is watched with the sound off by default (widely cited at 80-85%), so captions matter. <a href="https://digiday.com/media/silent-world-facebook-video/">[source]</a>
                            </blockquote>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Set your phone camera to 9:16 as the default.</li>
                              <li>Turn on auto-captions, then proofread before posting.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">11. Get sound and lighting right</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>People forgive a soft image and leave instantly over bad audio. A cheap clip-on mic beats an expensive camera in a noisy room. Then find a window: daylight on the face, phone at eye height, a background that&#x27;s comfortable to be seen in.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Use a clip-on mic, or hold the phone closer to your mouth.</li>
                              <li>Film facing a window, not with the light behind you.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">12. You don&#x27;t have to use video all the time</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Video doesn&#x27;t win hands down. Carousels and text posts are lower-friction, and currently among the best-rewarded formats on LinkedIn.</p>
                            <blockquote>Video posts average 5.6–5.9% engagement versus around 5.2% for image posts, both trailing carousel and document posts. <a href="https://www.socialinsider.io/social-media-benchmarks/linkedin">[source]</a>
                            </blockquote>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Start with a LinkedIn document or slide carousel if camera is your barrier.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">13. Batch content ahead of time</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Plan and create material in one sitting rather than relying on ongoing inspiration; batching is what protects consistency once diary pressure flares.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Block half a day once a month to film or write several pieces at once.</li>
                              <li>Schedule posts in advance using a scheduler.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">14. Apps that do 80% of the job are free</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>A phone, a free caption tool, and one editing app cover vertical crop, captions and basic trimming. CapCut and InShot both do the job - CapCut is better for cross-platform sharing.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Install one free editing app this week and cut a practice clip before you need one for real.</li>
                            </ul>
                            <p>In an academic study comparing procedural specialists at US academic training institutions, interventional cardiologists were more likely than interventional radiologists or vascular surgeons to already be functioning as online influencers (30.9% vs. 17.9% vs. 10.7%), and had the highest follower-to-following ratio of the three specialties.</p>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="height-2"></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="divider absolute top-0"></div>
          </section>
        </div>
        <div class="service-item">
          <section class="section padding-2 flex vertical align-center">
            <div class="container">
              <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                <div class="_5-col">
                  <div class="relative">
                    <h2 class="h3 padding-top padding-2">Distribution &amp; numbers</h2>
                    <div class="c_service-category-bg is-distribution"><img src="/images/Vector-3.svg" loading="lazy" alt=""></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="service-content">
                <div class="grid _13-col gap-5 t_flex t_vertical t_gap-2">
                  <div class="_5-col">
                    <p class="rise-section-tagline">Get seen by the right people</p>
                    <p>Algorithm, audience reach, proving it worked.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="_8-col">
                    <div class="rise-tips">
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">15. Listen, learn, interact, support</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Spend time commenting thoughtfully on others&#x27; posts before and after you start posting your own, and elevate other women&#x27;s work alongside yours. In a hierarchical field, that kind of peer amplification does real work - for the person amplified and for how you&#x27;re perceived.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Spend two weeks commenting on others&#x27; posts before posting anything of your own.</li>
                              <li>For every post you write, comment on or share one other woman&#x27;s post, and tag colleagues’ work.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">16. Consistency beats intensity</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>A post every fortnight for a year builds more than fifteen posts in one enthusiastic month followed by silence.</p>
                            <blockquote>Three-quarters of creators say algorithms punish creators who don&#x27;t post constantly. <a href="https://www.wpbeginner.com/research/creator-economy-statistics-that-will-blow-you-away/">[source]</a>
                            </blockquote>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Pick a realistic frequency you can sustain even in a busy on-call week.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">17. Protect your own time and headspace</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Set limits on engagement and be intentional about when you show up online. This is a wellbeing practice, not just a content strategy.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Set a fixed weekly time slot for social media.</li>
                              <li>Turn off notifications outside that window.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">18. Have a plan for pushback before you need it</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Deciding your approach in the moment is how people say something they regret or go silent for months. Decide now what earns a reply and who you call if it turns nasty.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Write down your own rules for pushback while you&#x27;re calm: what earns a reply, what gets muted.</li>
                              <li>Name one colleague you&#x27;d loop in if a pile-on gets personal.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">19. The cold-start problem</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Before you have numbers, growth is about direct outreach: sharing into group chats and engaging with people whose audience you want to reach.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>List ten people whose audience looks like the one you want.</li>
                              <li>Comment thoughtfully on their last three posts before you post your own.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
