                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>Most hospitals and universities have one, whether or not anyone&#x27;s mentioned it. It&#x27;s usually short and mostly common sense.</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Search your employer&#x27;s intranet for “social media policy” this week.</li>
                              <li>If nothing turns up, ask your comms or governance office directly.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="divider"></div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="rise-tip">
                        <div class="rise-tip-head">
                          <h3 class="rise-tip-title">22. Know what counts as identifiable, and get consent properly</h3>
                          <div class="rise-tip-arrow"></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rise-tip-body">
                          <div class="rise-tip-inner w-richtext">
                            <p>“Anonymised” gets defined generously by the poster and narrowly by everyone else. Get explicit consent before anything patient-related goes up, in writing, specific to the platform and use - not a verbal “they said it was fine.”</p>
                            <h5>Try this</h5>
                            <ul role="list">
                              <li>Ask: could this person recognise themselves from any detail here, not just the face?</li>
                              <li>If yes, get written consent or cut it.</li>
                            </ul>
                          </div>
